Z||000000||Trixi||02-16-2000||02:47 PM||||i have another problem<BR>i can't get any heat it cuts in and out <BR>and once in a while you will hear a bang come from the pipes running to the back of the car.<BR>today the temp light came on and the engin heated up<BR>is this caused from an air lock in the lines?<BR>or water pump?<P>thanks||||reg||11
Z||000001||DJRice||02-16-2000||03:39 PM||||It could be that you have some air in your cooling system, but the first thing to check would be the Thermostat.  Occasionally, those can come apart.  <P>To properly fill the cooling system, you must pull the cap off of the radiator and the thermostat housing so the air will have a place to escape.  Once fluid starts to flow from the radiator, cap it and continue to fill it from the thermo housing.  <P>If part of your thermostat is missing, then you need to do some serious praying, as a clog in the cooling system is bad business.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||02-16-2000||03:42 PM||||Make sure there is no air in the system. Could be with no coolant reaching the temp sensor, it gives a false reading. Pull the 'stat cap and the thermostat and make sure the coolant level is full. If not, fill it up to the top, put the cap back on(leave thermostat out).Run the engine for a minute or so. Keep filling it until it is full when you remove the cover. Then put the thermostat back in, and your set. This is assuming coolant level is the problem.<P>A bad water pump would have water dripping out through a weep hole on the bottom. Again, that is only if the coolant level is high enough to reach the hole.||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||02-16-2000||04:00 PM||||56,<P>Funny that we should be talking about coolant.  For an update, I don't have any air in the system and can't figure out how when I replaced much less fluid than came out.  I guess I should just be happy that everything works properly.  Also your question about engine heat up time.. my Duke heats up MUCH quicker than my 6.  The Duke gets above 100 (and can be felt by the HVAC) within a few minutes.  Probably a 4:10 minute ratio of Duke to 6.<P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||10
